_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 7
---
order: 7
---
short_label: X.7
---
statement: Incommensurable magnitudes do not have to one another the ratio which a number has to a number.
---
proof:

Let *A* and *B* be incommensurable magnitudes.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="160" height="100" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.7 diagram" src="propX7.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.7",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,30;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,30;0;0",
            "B0;point;free;-1000,65;0;0",
            "B3;point;free;1000,65;0;0",
            "A1;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0;0",
            "A2;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,130,0;0",
            "A;line;connect;A1,A2;black",
            "B1;point;lineSlider;B0,B3,30,0;0",
            "B2;point;lineSlider;B0,B3,109,0;0",
            "B;line;connect;B1,B2;black"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that *A* does not have to *B* the ratio which a number has to a number.

If *A* does have to *B* the ratio which a number has to a number, then *A* is commensurable with *B.* [!just X.6]

But it is not, therefore *A* does not have to *B* the ratio which a number has to a number.

Therefore, *incommensurable magnitudes do not have to one another the ratio which a number has to a number.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition is the contrapositive of the last one. It is used in @X.11.
